<%
  @menu_path = '/changes/menu'
%>

<%= page_title 'Upgrading Unpoly' %>

<div class="prose">
  <% auto_toc do %>

    <p>
      New Unpoly releases will occasionally rename or remove an existing function, event or HTML attribute.
      To assist with upgrading, <code>unpoly-migrate.js</code> polyfills changed APIs.
    </p>

    <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>

    <p>
      After upgrading Unpoly, add <code>unpoly-migrate.js</code> to your build.
      When your application calls an old API, a polyfill will
      forward the call to the new version automatically.
      A deprecation notice will be logged to your browser console:
    </p>

    <%= image_tag 'changes/log-deprecation.png', class: 'picture -border', width: 700, alt: 'The browser console logging a deprecation warning from unpoly-migrate.js' %>

    <p>
      You can now upgrade deprecated call sites by following the log.
      Your application will remain functional during the whole process.
    </p>

    <% admonition(:tip) do %>
      <p>
      <span class="line-on-wide">
        We recommend to temporarily load <code>unpoly-migrate.js</code> for every upgrade, even for minor version bumps.
      </span>
        <span class="line-on-wide">
        Changes handled by <code>unpoly-migrate.js</code> are not considered breaking changes.
      </span>
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <h2 id="workflow">Recommended upgrade workflow</h2>

    <p>
      We recommend these steps for every upgrade, including minor version bumps.
      They work the same whether a person or a coding agent does the upgrade.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <b>Find the version you're on.</b>
        Check <code>unpoly</code> in your <code>package.json</code>, <code>unpoly-rails</code> in your <code>Gemfile.lock</code>,
        the version in your CDN URL, or run <code>up.version</code> in your browser console.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Read the release notes of every version after yours,</b>
        up to the version you're upgrading to, in the <a href="/changes">list of all releases</a>.
        Changes marked with ⚠️ may require changes to your code, but <code>unpoly-migrate.js</code> polyfills them while you migrate.
        Changes marked with ❌ have no polyfill: update your code before you upgrade.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Upgrade Unpoly and load the polyfills</b>
        as described in <a href="#installation">Installing the polyfills</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Fix every deprecation warning.</b>
        Use your app and run its tests. Every call to an old API logs an error that names the new API.
        With good test coverage, you can <a href="#with-tests">let these errors fail your tests</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Remove <code>unpoly-migrate.js</code></b>
        once no warnings are left, or <a href="#permanent">keep it permanently</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <% if locals[:skill] %>
      <h3 id="releases-script">Listing the release notes for your upgrade</h3>

      <p>This skill includes a script that lists the release notes between two versions, in order:</p>

      <pre><code>python3 scripts/releases.py --from 2.7.2 --to 3.12.0</code></pre>

      <p>
        Add <code>--breaking</code> to also list each change marked with ⚠️ or ❌, with its line number.
        Leave out <code>--to</code> to upgrade to the latest version.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <h3 id="installation">Installing the polyfills</h3>

    <p><code>unpoly-migrate.js</code> comes included with Unpoly:</p>

    <table>
      <tr>
        <th>Development</th>
        <th colspan="2">Production</th>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <%= link_to_cdn_file('unpoly-migrate.js') %>
        </td>
        <td>
          <%= link_to_cdn_file('unpoly-migrate.min.js') %>
        </td>
        <td align="right" class="dimmed">
          <%= unpoly_library_size('unpoly-migrate.min.js') %> gzipped
        </td>
      </tr>
    </table>

    <p>Load <code>unpoly-migrate.js</code> <i>after</i> Unpoly, but <i>before</i> your own code:</p>

    <pre><code>&lt;script src="unpoly.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script src="unpoly-migrate.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt; &lt;!-- mark-line --&gt;
&lt;script src="app.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>

    <p>
      If you're using <a href="<%= doc_path('install', hash: 'npm') %>">Unpoly from npm</a>, import <code>unpoly-migrate.js</code> <i>after</i> Unpoly, but <i>before</i> your own code:
    </p>

    <pre><code>import 'unpoly/unpoly.js'
import 'unpoly/unpoly-migrate.js' // mark-line
import './your-scripts.js'
</code></pre>

    <h3 id="with-tests">Upgrading with automated tests</h3>


    <p>
      <code>unpoly-migrate.js</code> logs every call to a deprecated API to the browser's error console.
      If your end-to-end tests fail when the browser console shows an error, you can find the code that needs to be upgraded by following test failures.
      Most test runners can do this, e.g. with Playwright's <code>page.on('console')</code> or the browser logs in Selenium.
    </p>

    <p>
      To make the messages easier to read in your test logs, you can turn off their formatting:
    </p>

    <pre><code>up.log.config.format = false // log unformatted text for easier extraction</code></pre>

    <% admonition(:note) do %>
      <p>
        Before Unpoly 3.15, <code>unpoly-migrate.js</code> logged warnings.
        To log errors with an older version, set <code>up.migrate.config.logLevel = 'error'</code>.
      </p>
    <% end %>


    <h2 id="permanent">Keeping the polyfills permanently</h2>

    <p>
      If you prefer to upgrade at your own pace, it is possible to keep <code>unpoly-migrate.js</code> loaded permanently.
      This will add <%= unpoly_library_size('unpoly-migrate.min.js') %> (gzipped) to your production build.
    </p>

    <p>
      You can remove the deprecation messages from the browser log like so:
    </p>

    <pre><code>up.migrate.config.logLevel = 'none'</code></pre>

    <p>
      In an ES module bundle, set this in a module that you import right after <code>unpoly-migrate.js</code>.
      Imports run before other code in the same file, so the setting would otherwise come too late for deprecated configuration in your imported modules.
    </p>

    <% admonition(:note) do %>
      <p>
        While we have traditionally supported old APIs for years,
        polyfills may be dropped from <code>unpoly-migrate.js</code> in any major version.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <h2 id="coverage">Covered functionality</h2>

    <h3>Removed functions are polyfilled</h3>

    <p>
      If a function was removed without replacement, <code>unpoly-migrate.js</code> will provide a polyfill.
    </p>

    <p>
      E.g. Unpoly no longer supports <code>up.util.times()</code>, but the function is
      re-added by <code>unpoly-migrate.js</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>Renamed HTML attributes are aliased</h3>

    <p>
      E.g. <code>&lt;a up-close&gt;</code> will translate to <code>&lt;a up-dismiss&gt;</code>
    </p>

    <h3>Renamed functions are aliased</h3>

    <p>
      Calls to deprecated functions will be forwarded if there is an equivalent function
      in the current version.
    </p>

    <p>
      E.g. <code>up.modal.close()</code> will call <code>up.layer.dismiss()</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>Renamed options are aliased</h3>

    <p>
      Usage of deprecated options will be rewritten if there is an equivalent option
      in the current version.
    </p>

    <p>
      E.g. <code>{ reveal: false }</code> will be renamed to <code>{ scroll: false }</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>Renamed events are aliased</h3>

    <p>
      E.g.
      <code>up.on('up:proxy:load')</code> will bind to <code>up:request:load</code>.
    </p>

    <% admonition(:important) do %>
      <p>
        Event aliases are only used when registering listeners with <a href="<%= doc_path('up.on') %>"><code>up.on()</code></a>,
        but not with the native <code>Element#addEventListener()</code>.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <h3>Renamed packages are aliased</h3>

    <p>
      Usage of deprecated packages will be rewritten if there is an equivalent package
      in the current version.
    </p>

    <p>
      E.g. <code>up.proxy.config</code> will return <code>up.network.config</code>.
    </p>

<% end %>

</div>
